/* ===== Week 2 · story + theory ===== */
learn('t-waterfall', { ch: '2.1', title: 'The waterfall and its feedback', line: 'Riya runs a feasibility study, picks the waterfall for the depot portal and the V-model for the bus unit, and learns why a late-found defect costs so much.' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('2.1', 'The waterfall and its feedback', `
    ${NARR('Before anything else, Kulkarni sir sends Riya to Pune City Transport to find out whether Saarthi is worth doing at all.')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'I spent a day at PCT headquarters to understand what they want, and a day at Swargate depot to see what data actually exists: duty charts on paper, ticket machines, a radio room. I came back with three options: SMS alerts, display boards at stops, or the full app with GPS units.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'Costs, benefits, risks of each?')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'The app costs the most but it is the only one that also stops cash leakage. PCT\'s board said go.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'Good. That was the feasibility study, the first phase of the classical waterfall. Now: the depot portal replaces a paper process that has not changed in twenty years. The requirements will not move. Use the waterfall there. The bus unit is different.')}
    ${SAY('imran', 'The bus unit controls a GPS module and talks to the ticket machine. If it fails on the road, nobody can patch it from a laptop. I want every requirement to have a test planned before I write a line of code.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'Then you want the V-model.')}`));

  H(L, `<h2>1 · The classical waterfall</h2>
    <p>The <b>classical waterfall model</b> is the simplest and most intuitive life cycle model, and every other model is a modification of it. It divides the life cycle into six phases, done strictly one after another, like water falling down steps:</p>`);
  const WF = ['Feasibility study', 'Requirements\n& specification', 'Design', 'Coding &\nunit testing', 'Integration &\nsystem testing', 'Maintenance'], OUT = ['go / no-go', 'SRS document', 'design document', 'tested modules', 'tested system', 'working product'];
  stepper(L, 'Classical waterfall: one phase after another', SVG(660, 320, WF.map((t, i) => `<g${S(i)}>${bx(10 + i * 100, 14 + i * 46, 136, 42, t, { c: ['pur', 'blu', 'blu', 'grn', 'amb', 'red'][i], lh: 13 })}${tx(52 + i * 100, 66 + i * 46, OUT[i], { c: 'xs mu' })}</g>${i < 5 ? `<path d="M${146 + i * 100} ${35 + i * 46} q 24 0 24 30" class="ln b flow"${S(i + 1)} marker-end="url(#mkb)"/>` : ''}`).join('') + `<g${S(6)}>${pth('M 560 300 C 300 300, 40 300, 40 64', { c: 'dash a' })}${tx(230, 270, 'iterative waterfall adds feedback paths to earlier phases', { c: 'sm hot' })}</g>`, 'wide'),
    ['<b>Feasibility study</b>: is it financially worthwhile and technically feasible? Output: a go / no-go decision (and a business case).', '<b>Requirements analysis and specification</b>: find out exactly what the customer needs and write it down in the <b>SRS</b> (software requirements specification).', '<b>Design</b>: turn the requirements into a structure the programmers can implement.', '<b>Coding and unit testing</b>: write each module and test it on its own.', '<b>Integration and system testing</b>: put the modules together and test the whole system against the SRS.', '<b>Maintenance</b>: fix, adapt and enhance the delivered product for years.', 'In practice defects slip through, so the <b>iterative waterfall</b> adds feedback paths back to earlier phases (section 4).']);
  H(L, `<h3>Where does the effort go?</h3>
    <p>The phases between feasibility study and testing are called the <b>development phases</b>. Two facts are asked almost every year:</p>
    <div class="cols"><div>${SVG(360, 200, `<line x1="40" y1="170" x2="350" y2="170" class="ln"/>${[['Req.', 34], ['Design', 44], ['Coding', 40], ['Test', 64], ['Maint.', 140]].map(([t, v], i) => `<rect x="${52 + i * 60}" y="${170 - v}" width="40" height="${v}" rx="3" class="${i === 4 ? 'fill-r' : i === 3 ? 'fill-a' : 'fill-v'} bob"/>${tx(72 + i * 60, 186, t, { c: 'xs' })}`).join('')}${tx(190, 14, 'relative effort (illustrative)', { c: 'xs mu' })}`)}</div><div>
    <ul><li>Among <b>all</b> life cycle phases, <b>maintenance</b> consumes the maximum effort.</li><li>Among the <b>development</b> phases, <b>testing</b> consumes the maximum effort.</li></ul>
    <p>Organisations write their chosen model down as a <b>process model</b>: standards on the deliverables of every phase, entry and exit criteria for every phase, and methodologies for specification, design, testing and project management. Together these form the <b>organisation's software development methodology</b>, which fresh engineers are expected to master.</p></div></div>`);

  H(L, `<h2>2 · The feasibility study, step by step</h2>
    <p>The main aim is to decide whether developing the software is <b>financially worthwhile</b> and <b>technically feasible</b>. Feasibility has three dimensions: <b>economic</b> (cost/benefit), <b>technical</b> and <b>schedule</b>. A project proceeds only if all three are satisfied.</p>
    ${STEPS(['<b>Roughly understand what the customer wants</b>: the data that would be input to the system, the processing needed on that data, the output data to be produced, and constraints on the system\'s behaviour.', '<b>Work out an overall understanding</b> of the problem.', '<b>Formulate different solution strategies.</b>', '<b>Examine the alternatives</b> in terms of resources required, cost of development and development time.', '<b>Perform a cost-benefit analysis</b> to pick the best solution. You may also find that <b>no</b> solution is feasible: high cost, resource constraints or technical reasons.'])}
    ${IDEA('The lecture\'s case study: SPF scheme for CFL', 'CFL (a mining company) has more than 50,000 employees, mostly casual labourers. Mining is risky and casualties are high; there is a provident fund, but settlement takes too long, so a special provident fund (SPF) system is needed for faster disbursement. The manager visits the main office to find the required functions, visits a mine site to find what data can be input, suggests alternative solutions, picks the best and presents it to CFL\'s officials for a <b>go / no-go decision</b>. Riya did the same at PCT headquarters and Swargate depot.')}
    <h3>Cost-benefit analysis and the business case</h3>
    <p>Identify all <b>costs</b> (development, set-up, operational), identify the value of <b>benefits</b> (quantifiable and non-quantifiable), and check that benefits exceed costs. The feasibility study should produce a <b>business case</b> that justifies starting the project, shows benefits exceeding costs and takes account of business risks. An effective business case has six parts: executive summary · project background (what exactly the project undertakes, not the bigger picture) · business opportunity (what difference it makes, and what if we don't do it) · costs (development, implementation, training, change management, operations) · benefits (usually revenue generation and cost reduction) · risks and how they will be managed. Chapter 3 puts numbers on all of this.</p>`);

  H(L, `<h2>3 · Strengths and weaknesses of the waterfall</h2>
    ${VS([['Strengths', '', ['Easy to understand and use, even by inexperienced staff', 'Milestones are well understood by the team', 'Requirements stability during development', 'Strong management control: plan, staff, track']], ['Deficiencies', '', ['All requirements must be known up front, yet in most projects they change after the start', 'Can give a false impression of progress', 'Integration is one big bang at the end', 'Little chance for the customer to preview the system']], ['When to use it', '', ['Requirements are well known and stable', 'Technology is understood', 'The team has experience with similar projects']]])}
    <p>Saarthi's depot portal ticks all three boxes, so Riya uses the waterfall there.</p>
    ${ANALOGY('Even when a project did not really follow the waterfall, its <b>documents</b> should read as if it did, because that makes them easy to understand. A mathematician presents a proof as a single clean chain of deductions, even though it came from a convoluted set of partial attempts, blind alleys and backtracks.')}`);

  H(L, `<h2>4 · Iterative waterfall and phase containment</h2>
    <p>The classical model is <b>idealistic</b>: it assumes no defect is ever introduced during any activity. In practice defects get introduced in almost every phase and are usually <b>detected much later</b>. Watch what happened to one Saarthi requirement:</p>`);
  const PH = ['Requirements', 'Design', 'Coding', 'Testing'];
  stepper(L, 'A defect injected early, found late', SVG(640, 230, PH.map((t, i) => `<g${HL(...(i === 0 ? [1, 4] : [4]))}>${bx(20 + i * 155, 70, 135, 50, t, { c: 'blu' })}</g>${i < 3 ? ar(155 + i * 155, 95, 173 + i * 155, 95) : ''}`).join('') +
    `<g${S(1)}><circle cx="87" cy="58" r="11" class="fill-r"/>${tx(87, 58, '!', { c: 'wh' })}${tx(87, 30, 'fare stages misread', { c: 'xs red' })}</g>
    <g${S(2)}>${tx(242, 150, 'design built on the wrong rule', { c: 'xs red' })}</g><g${S(3)}>${tx(397, 150, 'code written for it', { c: 'xs red' })}</g>
    <g${S(4)}><circle cx="552" cy="58" r="11" class="fill-a"/>${tx(552, 58, '✓', {})}${tx(552, 30, 'detected here', { c: 'xs hot' })}</g>
    <g${S(5)}>${pth('M545 130 C 480 210, 160 210, 88 124', { c: 'r flow' })}${tx(320, 214, 'rework: SRS + design + code + tests', { c: 'sm red' })}</g>`),
    ['Four phases of the depot portal.', 'Dev misreads how fare stages are counted. The defect enters the <b>requirements</b> document.', 'Nobody notices; the <b>design</b> is built on the wrong rule.', 'The <b>code</b> faithfully implements the wrong design.', 'System <b>testing</b> finally catches it, three phases after it was introduced.', 'Now the SRS, the design, the code and the test cases must all be reworked. <b>The later the phase in which a defect is detected, the more expensive its removal</b>, because every phase in between must be redone. Detecting errors as close as possible to their point of introduction is the principle of <b>phase containment of errors</b>.']);
  H(L, `<p>So the <b>iterative waterfall model</b> keeps the same phases but adds <b>feedback paths</b> from every phase to the earlier ones: once a defect is detected, the phase in which it occurred is reworked, and some of the work of all later phases is redone. Reviews at the end of every phase (week 8) are the cheapest way to achieve phase containment.</p>`);
  choice(L, 'A design defect in the bus unit is found during <b>design review</b>, another during <b>system testing</b>. Which statement is right?', [
    ['Both cost the same to fix: it is the same kind of defect.', 'Cost depends on how many phases the defect survived.', false],
    ['The one found in system testing is far more expensive: design, code and unit and integration test work must be redone.', 'Right: phase containment of errors. Catch defects in the phase that created them.', true],
    ['The one found in review is more expensive because reviews take a whole team.', 'A review costs a few hours; the late fix cascades through several phases.', false]]);

  H(L, `<h2>5 · The V-model: plan the test while you build</h2>
    <p>The <b>V-model</b> is a variant of the waterfall that emphasises <b>verification and validation</b> (V&V). V&V activities are spread over the whole life cycle: in every development phase, the matching <b>test activities are planned in parallel</b>. Imran uses it for the in-bus unit.</p>`);
  const vL = [['Requirements\nspecification', 60, 60], ['High-level\ndesign', 120, 120], ['Detailed\ndesign', 180, 180]], vR = [['System\ntesting', 520, 60], ['Integration\ntesting', 460, 120], ['Unit\ntesting', 400, 180]];
  stepper(L, 'The V-model, phase by phase', SVG(660, 330, `
    ${bx(10, 6, 130, 36, 'Project planning', { c: 'pur', at: S(0) })}${bx(500, 4, 150, 40, 'Production, operation\n& maintenance', { c: 'pur', at: S(8), lh: 13 })}
    ${vL.map(([t, x, y], i) => bx(x - 30, y, 130, 44, t, { c: 'blu', at: S(i + 1), lh: 13 })).join('')}
    ${vR.map(([t, x, y], i) => bx(x - 30, y, 130, 44, t, { c: 'grn', at: S(7 - i), lh: 13 })).join('')}
    ${vL.map(([t, x, y], i) => `<line x1="${x + 100}" y1="${y + 22}" x2="${vR[i][1] - 30}" y2="${y + 22}" class="ln dash a"${S(i + 1)}/>${tx((x + 100 + vR[i][1] - 30) / 2, y + 12, ['system test design', 'integration test design', 'unit test design'][i], { c: 'xs hot', at: S(i + 1) })}`).join('')}
    ${bx(265, 250, 130, 44, 'Coding', { c: 'amb', at: S(4) })}
    ${pth('M95 104 L 125 118', { at: S(2) })}${pth('M155 164 L 185 178', { at: S(3) })}${pth('M215 224 L 268 252', { at: S(4) })}
    ${pth('M392 252 L 440 226', { at: S(5) })}${pth('M470 178 L 498 166', { at: S(6) })}${pth('M530 118 L 558 106', { at: S(7) })}
    ${tx(150, 318, 'verification ↓', { c: 'sm blu' })}${tx(510, 318, '↑ validation', { c: 'sm grn' })}`, 'wide'),
    ['<b>Project planning</b> starts the V.', '<b>Requirements analysis and specification</b>, and at the same time <b>system test design</b>: every requirement gets the test that will prove it.', '<b>High-level design</b>, with <b>integration test design</b> planned alongside.', '<b>Detailed design</b>, with <b>unit test design</b>.', '<b>Coding</b> at the bottom of the V.', 'Climbing the right arm: <b>unit testing</b>, using the unit tests designed earlier.', '<b>Integration testing</b> against the high-level design.', '<b>System testing</b> against the requirements specification.', '<b>Production, operation and maintenance.</b> Every deliverable was testable from the day it was written.']);
  H(L, `${VS([['V-model strengths', '', ['Emphasises planning for V&V from the early stages', 'Each deliverable is made testable', 'Easy to use']], ['V-model weaknesses', '', ['No overlapping of phases', 'Does not handle iterations', 'Does not easily accommodate later changes to requirements', 'No support for effective risk handling']], ['When to use it', '', ['Natural choice for systems requiring <b>high reliability</b>: embedded control applications, safety-critical software', 'All requirements known up front', 'Solution and technology known']]])}`);
  choice(L, 'Which Saarthi component is the natural fit for the V-model?', [
    ['The passenger app, whose screens PCT keeps changing', 'Changing requirements are exactly what the V-model handles badly.', false],
    ['The in-bus embedded unit, which must be highly reliable and has fixed requirements', 'Right: embedded control and safety-critical software is the textbook V-model case.', true],
    ['A research spike on predicting bus arrival times', 'That is risky and exploratory: a spiral or prototype fits better (next chapter).', false]], { tag: 'Quick check' });
  quickcheck(L, [['Which phase takes the most effort overall? Among development phases?', 'Maintenance overall; testing among the development phases.'], ['What is phase containment of errors?', 'Detecting (and fixing) an error as close as possible to the phase in which it was introduced, because the later it is detected the more phases must be reworked.'], ['Name the three dimensions of feasibility.', 'Economic (cost-benefit), technical, schedule.']]);
  H(L, RECAP('Waterfall: feasibility → requirements (SRS) → design → coding & unit test → integration & system test → maintenance. Simple, strong control, but needs stable requirements and gives late, big-bang integration.', 'Effort: maintenance largest overall; testing largest among development phases.', 'Feasibility: economic, technical, schedule; understand inputs, processing, outputs, constraints; alternatives; CBA; go/no-go; business case.', 'Iterative waterfall = feedback paths; phase containment: catch errors in the phase that made them.', 'V-model: test design in parallel with each development phase; for high-reliability embedded and safety-critical systems; poor at change and risk.'));
  H(L, NEXT('The portal and the bus unit have their models. The passenger app is another story: Mr Bhosale cannot say what he wants until he sees it.', 't-proto', 'Chapter 2.2 · Show them before you build it'));
});

learn('t-proto', { ch: '2.2', title: 'Show them before you build it', line: 'A throw-away prototype for the app, incremental delivery ranked by value/cost, RAD for reports, evolutionary releases, and the spiral for the risky GPS work.' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('2.2', 'Show them before you build it', `
    ${SAY('bho', 'I want an app that is simple for a vegetable vendor and powerful for a college student. You are the experts. Show me something.')}
    ${SAY('riya', '"Show me something." So we cannot write an SRS yet.')}
    ${SAY('pooja', 'Give me a week. I\'ll make a clickable app with fake buses: the arrival time comes from a table, not a real calculation.')}
    ${NARR('A week later conductors and passengers tap through Pooja\'s prototype at Swargate. Half the ticket flow gets redesigned in one afternoon.')}
    ${SAY('bho', 'Now I understand what I want. And I want something on three routes before the festival season.')}
    ${SAY('imran', 'One more worry: GPS inside the old city lanes. If the signal drops between tall buildings, the whole idea of live tracking fails. I can\'t promise anything until I\'ve tested it.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'Four problems, four tools: a prototype for unclear requirements, increments for early delivery, quick RAD-style cycles for the reports, and the spiral for risk.')}`));

  H(L, `<h2>1 · The prototyping model</h2>
    <p>A derivative of the waterfall: <b>before</b> actual development starts, build a working <b>prototype</b>. A prototype is a <b>toy implementation</b> of the system: limited functional capabilities, low reliability, inefficient performance. Why build something that bad on purpose?</p>
    ${COLS(`<h4>To show the customer</h4><ul><li>input data formats, messages, reports, interactive dialogs</li><li>"I'll know it when I see it" requirements</li></ul>`, `<h4>To examine technical issues</h4><ul><li>major design decisions often depend on things like the <b>response time of a hardware controller</b> or the <b>efficiency of a sorting algorithm</b></li></ul>`, `<h4>Because nobody gets it right first time</h4><ul><li>"plan to throw away the first version if we want to develop good software"</li></ul>`)}
    <p>Other reasons the slides list: learning by doing (requirements only partially known), improved communication, improved user involvement, reduced need for documentation, reduced maintenance costs.</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'The prototyping loop', SVG(660, 250, `
    ${bx(10, 20, 120, 46, 'Requirements\ngathering', { c: 'blu', at: S(0) })}${ar(130, 43, 152, 43, { at: S(1) })}${bx(154, 20, 100, 46, 'Quick\ndesign', { c: 'blu', at: S(1) })}${ar(254, 43, 276, 43, { at: S(1) })}
    ${bx(278, 20, 110, 46, 'Build\nprototype', { c: 'amb', at: S(1) })}${ar(388, 43, 410, 43, { at: S(2) })}${bx(412, 20, 130, 46, 'Customer\nevaluation', { c: 'pur', at: S(2) })}
    <g${S(3)}>${pth('M477 66 C 477 120, 220 120, 204 70', { c: 'a flow' })}${tx(340, 132, 'refine requirements, iterate', { c: 'xs hot' })}</g>
    <g${S(4)}>${ar(542, 43, 570, 43)}${tx(600, 36, 'customer', { c: 'xs grn' })}${tx(600, 50, 'satisfied', { c: 'xs grn' })}</g>
    ${['Design', 'Implement', 'Test', 'Maintain'].map((t, i) => bx(40 + i * 150, 170, 120, 40, t, { c: 'grn', at: S(5) })).join('')}${[0, 1, 2].map(i => ar(160 + i * 150, 190, 188 + i * 150, 190, { at: S(5) })).join('')}
    <g${S(5)}>${pth('M610 66 C 640 120, 120 120, 100 166', { c: 'g' })}${tx(330, 236, 'the real system, built with the waterfall; the prototype\'s code is thrown away', { c: 'xs mu' })}</g>`, 'wide'),
    ['Start with <b>approximate requirements</b>.', 'Carry out a <b>quick design</b> and build the prototype using short-cuts: inefficient, inaccurate or dummy functions; a <b>table look-up</b> instead of the actual computation (Pooja\'s fake arrival times).', 'Submit the prototype to the customer for <b>evaluation</b>.', 'Based on the feedback, refine the requirements and the prototype. The cycle repeats until the user approves.', 'The customer is satisfied.', 'Now the actual system is developed using the <b>waterfall</b>. The requirements phase becomes almost redundant: the final prototype is an <b>animated requirements specification</b>. The prototype\'s design and code are usually thrown away, but the experience gained helps a great deal.']);
  H(L, `<p><b>Does the extra prototype make the project more expensive?</b> It costs something, but the <b>overall</b> development cost is usually <b>lower</b> for systems with <b>unclear user requirements</b> and systems with <b>unresolved technical issues</b>: those requirements and issues get settled early, instead of appearing later as change requests that need massive redesign.</p>
    ${VS([['Advantages', '', ['More usable software', 'User needs better accommodated', 'Higher-quality design', 'Easier to maintain', 'Overall, less cost']], ['Disadvantages', '', ['Expensive for some projects', 'Susceptible to <b>over-engineering</b>: designers add the sophistication they could not put in the prototype']]])}`);

  H(L, `<h2>2 · Why waterfall-based models struggle</h2>
    <p>All the models so far determine and document the requirements at the start and assume they are fixed from that point, with long-term plans built on them. Three difficulties follow: <b>change requests are hard to accommodate</b> (about <b>40 %</b> of requirements change during development: Capers Jones's study of 8,000 projects found 40 % of final requirements arrived after development had begun), <b>custom applications are costly</b>, and the processes are <b>heavyweight</b>.</p>
    ${IDEA('Frederick Brooks', '"… the assumption that one can specify a satisfactory system in advance, get bids for its construction, have it built, and install it … is fundamentally wrong, and many software acquisition problems spring from this."')}`);

  H(L, `<h2>3 · The incremental model</h2>
    <p>Victor Basili: start with a simple implementation of a subset of the requirements and iteratively enhance the evolving versions, taking advantage of what was learned from developing and <b>using</b> the earlier versions. This <b>incremental and iterative development (IID)</b> builds the system in a number of iterations, each producing a <b>working program</b>. It manages change well and is the foundation of agile techniques, the Rational Unified Process (RUP) and Extreme Programming (XP).</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'What the customer sees: waterfall vs incremental', SVG(640, 220, `
    ${tx(110, 18, 'Waterfall', { c: 'sm mu' })}${tx(430, 18, 'Incremental', { c: 'sm mu' })}
    <g${S(3)}>${bx(40, 120, 140, 70, 'A B C\n(everything, once)', { c: 'blu' })}</g>${tx(110, 100, 'nothing … nothing …', { c: 'xs mu', at: U(3) })}
    ${bx(280, 150, 60, 40, 'A', { c: 'grn', at: S(0) })}${tx(310, 206, 'release 1', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(0) })}
    <g${S(1)}>${bx(370, 150, 60, 40, 'A', { c: 'grn' })}${bx(370, 110, 60, 40, 'B', { c: 'amb' })}${tx(400, 206, 'release 2', { c: 'xs mu' })}</g>
    <g${S(2)}>${bx(460, 150, 60, 40, 'A', { c: 'grn' })}${bx(460, 110, 60, 40, 'B', { c: 'amb' })}${bx(460, 70, 60, 40, 'C', { c: 'pur' })}${tx(490, 206, 'release 3', { c: 'xs mu' })}</g>`),
    ['Incremental: the <b>first increment</b> delivers the core functionality (A).', '<b>Successive increments</b> add (or fix) functionality: A + B.', 'The <b>final increment</b> is the complete product.', 'The waterfall gives one single release at the end. Each incremental iteration is a short mini-project with its own life cycle (e.g. a mini waterfall), and feedback from using A shapes B.']);
  H(L, `<p>The requirements are split into <b>features</b> ("slices"), an outline design is made, and then each increment is developed, validated and integrated, with the system validated after each one. The <b>planned incremental delivery</b> process: identify system objectives → plan increments → incremental delivery plan → (design increment → build → implement → evaluate → feedback) repeated for each increment.</p>
    <h3>Which increment first? Value-to-cost ratio</h3>
    <p>Some steps are prerequisites because of physical dependencies; the rest can be ordered by the <b>value-to-cost ratio V/C</b>, where V is a score 1-10 for the value to the customer and C a score 0-10 for the cost to the developers. Highest ratio first. The lecture's example:</p>
    ${TBL(['Step', 'Value', 'Cost', 'V/C', 'Order'], [['profit reports', 9, 2, '4.5', '2nd'], ['online database', 1, 9, '0.11', '5th'], ['ad hoc enquiry', 5, 5, '1', '4th'], ['purchasing plans', 9, 4, '2.25', '3rd'], ['profit-based pay for managers', 9, 1, '<b>9</b>', '<b>1st</b>']])}`);
  choice(L, 'Saarthi\'s first increment for the festival. Values/costs: live bus position (V 9, C 3), online ticketing (V 8, C 6), trip-history export (V 2, C 4), favourite stops (V 5, C 1). Which goes first?', [
    ['Live bus position, the highest value', 'V/C = 3. Value alone is not the rule.', false],
    ['Favourite stops: V/C = 5, the highest ratio', 'Right: rank by V/C. Favourite stops (5) → live position (3) → ticketing (1.33) → export (0.5), unless a physical dependency forces another order.', true],
    ['Online ticketing, the most expensive, to get it out of the way', 'The incremental model delivers the best value per unit of cost first.', false]]);

  H(L, `<h2>4 · RAD: rapid application development</h2>
    <p>Sometimes called the <b>rapid prototyping model</b>. Its aims: <b>decrease the time and cost</b> of development, and accommodate change requests <b>as early as possible</b>, before large investments in development and testing. The underlying principle: <b>make only short-term plans and reuse existing code heavily</b>.</p>
    ${STEPS(['Plans are made for one increment at a time. The time planned for each iteration is a <b>time box</b>.', 'In each iteration a quick-and-dirty, prototype-style version of some selected functionality is built.', 'The customer evaluates it and gives feedback; the prototype is refined. Each iteration enhances the functionality a little.'])}
    <p>RAD is fast because of <b>specialised tools</b> with a visual style of development (drag and drop), reusable components and standard APIs.</p>
    ${VS([['RAD suits', '', ['Performance and reliability are not critical', 'The system can be split into several independent modules']], ['RAD does not suit', '', ['Few plug-in components available', 'High performance or reliability required', 'No precedent for similar products', 'The system cannot be modularised']], ['Prototyping vs RAD', '', ['Prototyping: the prototype gives insight, chooses between alternatives, elicits feedback, and is usually <b>thrown away</b>', 'RAD: the prototype is <b>refined into the final software</b>']]])}
    <p>Saarthi's depot reports (trips per route, revenue per day) are a good RAD candidate: low criticality, independent modules, a reporting tool to drag and drop with.</p>`);

  H(L, `<h2>5 · The evolutionary model with iteration</h2>
    <p>It recognises the reality of changing requirements and promotes <b>early risk mitigation</b>: break the system into mini-projects and tackle the riskier issues first: "<b>plan a little, design a little, code a little</b>". End users, testers, integrators and technical writers are involved early. Requirements, plans, estimates and solution <b>evolve</b> over the iterations instead of being frozen up front (Craig Larman); a complex system succeeds best in small steps, with the option to retreat to a previous successful step and get real-world feedback before committing all resources (Tom Gilb).</p>`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 170, `${bx(10, 60, 110, 50, 'Initial rough\nrequirements', { c: 's2' })}${ar(120, 85, 146, 85)}${bx(148, 60, 100, 50, 'Initial\nversion', { c: 'blu' })}${ar(248, 85, 274, 85)}${bx(276, 60, 120, 50, 'Intermediate\nversions', { c: 'amb' })}${ar(396, 85, 422, 85)}${bx(424, 60, 100, 50, 'Final\nversion', { c: 'grn' })}
    ${pth('M336 60 C 336 10, 276 10, 266 56', { c: 'a flow' })}${tx(300, 18, 'specification · development · validation, repeated', { c: 'xs hot' })}
    ${tx(320, 140, 'core modules first; each iteration = a mini waterfall ending in tested, integrated, executable code', { c: 'xs mu' })}`));
  H(L, `<ul><li>Develop the <b>core modules</b> first; refine the skeletal software into increasing levels of capability by adding new functionality in successive versions (iterations).</li><li>Iterations are <b>short and fixed</b>: usually <b>2 to 6 weeks</b>, with many of them (for example 10-15). Each ends with a tested, integrated, executable system.</li><li>Requirements are not frozen: requirements and design can both be modified. A new release may add functions and enhance existing ones.</li></ul>
    ${VS([['Advantages', '', ['Users experiment with a partial system early → exact requirements found', 'Core modules tested thoroughly', 'Complexity managed one increment at a time', 'Changing requirements handled', 'Customer feedback incorporated efficiently', 'Training can start on an early release', 'Frequent releases fix unexpected problems quicker']], ['Problems', '', ['The process is <b>unpredictable</b>: hard to schedule and staff', 'Systems tend to be <b>poorly structured</b>: continual changes degrade the structure', 'Systems may <b>never converge</b> to a final version']]])}`);

  H(L, `<h2>6 · The spiral model: risk drives everything</h2>
    <p>Proposed by <b>Barry Boehm in 1988</b>, its main feature is <b>risk handling</b>. Each loop of the spiral is a phase of the software process, in which one or more features are taken up, elaborated, their risks handled, and implemented. There are <b>no fixed phases</b>: the loops shown are just examples. Follow Imran's GPS problem around two loops:</p>`);
  const cx = 320, cy = 180;
  const seg = (k) => { const a0 = 180 + k * 90, pts = []; for (let d = 0; d <= 90; d += 6) { const a = (a0 + d) * Math.PI / 180, r = 14 + (k * 90 + d) * 0.17; pts.push(`${(cx + r * Math.cos(a)).toFixed(1)},${(cy + r * Math.sin(a)).toFixed(1)}`); } return `<polyline points="${pts.join(' ')}" class="ln thick p"${S(k + 1)}/>`; };
  stepper(L, 'Two loops of the spiral', SVG(640, 362, `<line x1="${cx}" y1="20" x2="${cx}" y2="340" class="grid"/><line x1="70" y1="${cy}" x2="570" y2="${cy}" class="grid"/>
    ${[['1 · Determine objectives,\nalternatives, risks', 150, 40, 'blu'], ['2 · Identify & resolve\nrisks', 490, 40, 'red'], ['3 · Develop & validate\nnext level of product', 490, 330, 'grn'], ['4 · Customer evaluation,\nreview & plan next loop', 150, 330, 'amb']].map(([t, x, y, c], i) => `<g${HL(i + 1, i + 5)}>${bx(x - 100, y - 22, 200, 44, t, { c, lh: 13 })}</g>`).join('')}
    ${[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7].map(seg).join('')}<circle cx="${cx}" cy="${cy}" r="4" class="fill-p"/>`, 'wide'),
    ['Start at the centre with the riskiest open question: "Will GPS tracking work in the old city?"', '<b>Quadrant 1, objective setting</b>: identify the objectives of the phase, examine the risks associated with them (a <b>risk</b> is any adverse circumstance that might hamper successful completion), and find alternative solutions: plain GPS, GPS + mobile-tower positioning, GPS + odometer dead-reckoning.', '<b>Quadrant 2, risk assessment and reduction</b>: analyse each risk in detail and take steps to reduce it. Imran builds a cheap test unit and rides route 5 through Kasba Peth for a week. (If the risk were inappropriate requirements, a prototype would be the reduction step.)', '<b>Quadrant 3, development and validation</b>: develop and validate the next level of the product: GPS + odometer dead-reckoning firmware.', '<b>Quadrant 4, review and planning</b>: review the results with the customer and plan the next iteration. PCT agrees to the hybrid approach.', 'Loop 2, quadrant 1: next objectives: reporting position to the server every 10 seconds over a patchy mobile network.', 'Quadrant 2: risk of data loss when the network drops → store-and-forward buffer prototype.', 'Quadrant 3: build and validate the communication module.', 'Quadrant 4: review and plan. With each loop a progressively more complete version of the software is built.'], { ms: 3600 });
  H(L, `<h3>The spiral as a meta-model</h3>
    <p>The spiral <b>subsumes</b> all the models discussed: a <b>single loop</b> of the spiral is the <b>waterfall</b>; its loops are <b>evolutionary</b> levels; it uses <b>prototyping</b> as a risk-reduction mechanism; and it retains the <b>step-wise approach</b> of the waterfall. It lets the team understand and react to risks in every iteration.</p>
    ${TBL(['Model', 'Key idea', 'Best when'], [['Prototyping', 'Throw-away toy version first, then waterfall', 'Unclear requirements or unresolved technical issues'], ['Incremental', 'Core first, then increments by V/C', 'Early partial delivery; novice teams'], ['RAD', 'Time-boxed prototypes refined into the product; tools + reuse', 'Non-critical, modular systems, fast'], ['Evolutionary', 'Iterations of 2-6 weeks; requirements evolve', 'High uncertainty, changing requirements'], ['Spiral', 'Each loop: objectives → risk → develop → plan', 'High-risk projects; meta-model']])}`);
  quickcheck(L, [['Why is the prototype usually thrown away, and what survives?', 'It was built with short-cuts (dummy functions, look-up tables) and low quality; what survives is the approved requirements (an animated specification) and the team\'s experience.'], ['What are the four quadrants of a spiral loop?', '1 Determine objectives (and risks, alternatives); 2 identify and resolve risks; 3 develop and validate the next level; 4 customer evaluation, review and plan the next loop.'], ['Prototyping vs RAD in one line?', 'Prototyping throws the prototype away; RAD refines the prototype into the final product.']]);
  H(L, RECAP('Prototype = toy implementation (limited function, low reliability, inefficient) to settle unclear requirements or technical issues; thrown away; lowers overall cost for such systems; risk of over-engineering.', '~40 % of requirements change after development starts (Capers Jones, 8,000 projects) → waterfall-based models struggle.', 'Incremental (Basili): core first, increments ranked by V/C; each iteration a working program.', 'RAD: time boxes, short-term plans, heavy reuse, visual tools; for non-critical modular systems; prototype refined, not thrown away.', 'Evolutionary: 2-6 week iterations, requirements evolve; unpredictable, may degrade structure or never converge.', 'Spiral (Boehm 1988): risk-driven loops, four quadrants, meta-model of all the others.'));
  H(L, NEXT('The pilot is live. Then PCT changes its priorities every fortnight: a festival, a new route, a fare revision. Riya needs a process built for change.', 't-agile', 'Chapter 2.3 · Agile, XP and Scrum'));
});

learn('t-agile', { ch: '2.3', title: 'Built for change: agile, XP and Scrum', line: 'The app team switches to Scrum: product owner, Scrum master, sprints, daily scrums and burndown charts; XP takes good practices to the extreme.' }, L => {
  H(L, STORY('2.3', 'Built for change', `
    ${SAY('dev', 'PCT has changed the app priorities three times this month. First the festival routes, then women\'s safety alerts, now student passes.')}
    ${SAY('riya', 'And each change means rewriting the plan and the SRS. We spend more time on documents than on the app.')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'Stop fighting the change. The app team is six people in one room with a customer who changes his mind. That is exactly what agile methods are for. Dev, you will be the product owner: you decide what matters most to PCT. Riya, you become the Scrum master: you protect the team and clear its obstacles. One sprint a month. No changes inside a sprint.')}
    ${SAY('pooja', 'And no fat SRS?')}
    ${SAY('kul', 'Working software is the measure of progress. Write only the documents someone truly needs.')}`));

  H(L, `<h2>1 · What "agile" means</h2>
    <p><b>Agile</b>: easily moved, light, nimble, active. Agility is achieved by <b>fitting the process to the project</b> and <b>avoiding things that waste time</b>. The agile model was proposed in the <b>mid-1990s</b> to overcome the shortcomings of the waterfall, primarily to help projects <b>handle change requests</b>. The requirements are decomposed into many small parts that are developed incrementally.</p>
    <h3>The Agile Manifesto</h3><p>Four value statements. The items on the left are valued <b>more</b> than those on the right (the right side is not worthless):</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Agile manifesto: what is valued more', SVG(640, 240, [['Individuals and interactions', 'processes and tools'], ['Working software', 'comprehensive documentation'], ['Customer collaboration', 'contract negotiation'], ['Responding to change', 'following a plan']].map(([a, b2], i) => `<g${S(i)}>${bx(20, 12 + i * 56, 270, 44, a, { c: 'grn' })}${tx(320, 34 + i * 56, 'over', { c: 'sm mu' })}${bx(350, 12 + i * 56, 270, 44, b2, { c: 's2' })}</g>`).join('')),
    ['<b>Individuals and interactions</b> over processes and tools.', '<b>Working software</b> over comprehensive documentation.', '<b>Customer collaboration</b> over contract negotiation.', '<b>Responding to change</b> over following a plan. Agile methodologies built on these values: <b>XP, Scrum</b>, Unified Process, Crystal, DSDM, Lean.']);
  H(L, `<h3>How an agile project actually runs</h3>
    ${COLS(DEF('User stories', 'Requirements written as short stories, simpler than use cases.') + DEF('Metaphor', 'Based on the user stories, developers propose a common vision of what is required.'), DEF('Spike', 'A simple program written to explore potential solutions.') + DEF('Refactor', 'Restructure code without changing its behaviour.'))}
    <ul><li>Only <b>one increment</b> is planned, developed and deployed at a time. <b>No long-term plans</b> are made.</li><li>Even if an iteration adds little functionality, a new release is still made at its end and delivered to the customer for regular use.</li><li><b>Face-to-face communication</b> is favoured over written documents: the team shares one office and is deliberately kept <b>small (5-9 people)</b>. This makes agile most suited to <b>small</b> projects.</li><li>The primary measure of progress is the <b>incremental release of working software</b>. Frequent delivery (every few weeks), easy accommodation of change, close customer-developer cooperation.</li></ul>`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 250, `<line x1="60" y1="210" x2="610" y2="210" class="ln"/><line x1="60" y1="210" x2="60" y2="20" class="ln"/>${tx(335, 236, 'richness of communication channel: cold → hot', { c: 'xs mu' })}${tx(30, 115, 'effec-\ntiveness', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    <path d="M80 196 C 250 180, 420 120, 590 34" class="ln thick g draw" style="--len:640"/>
    ${[['paper', 90, 194], ['audiotape', 170, 186], ['email', 245, 176], ['videotape', 310, 164], ['phone', 380, 144], ['video call', 450, 116], ['face-to-face', 520, 82], ['face-to-face at whiteboard', 560, 50]].map(([t, x, y]) => `<circle cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="4.5" class="fill-g"/>${tx(x, y - 13, t, { c: 'xs' })}`).join('')}
    ${tx(200, 40, 'Cockburn / Ambler: documentation options are "cold",\nconversation options are "hot"', { c: 'xs mu' })}`));
  H(L, `<h3>Travel light: agile documentation</h3>
    <p>"You need far less documentation than you think." Agile documents are concise, describe information less likely to change and "good things to know", and are sufficiently accurate, consistent and detailed. Valid reasons to document: <b>stakeholders require it</b>, to <b>define a contract model</b>, to <b>support communication with an external group</b>, and to <b>think something through</b>.</p>
    <h3>Requirements as a priority stack</h3>`);
  stepper(L, 'Agile requirements management', SVG(640, 300, `${tx(100, 16, '↑ high priority', { c: 'xs grn' })}${tx(100, 268, '↓ low priority', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    ${['live position', 'buy ticket', 'festival routes', 'favourite stops', 'fare history', 'dark mode'].map((t, i) => `<g${U(i < 2 ? 1 : 99)}>${bx(20, 28 + i * 38, 160, 32, t, { c: i < 2 ? 'grn' : 'blu', r: 4 })}</g>`).join('')}
    <g${S(1)}>${bx(300, 40, 180, 70, 'Iteration 1 builds\nthe top items', { c: 'grn' })}${ar(184, 58, 296, 70, { c: 'g' })}</g>
    <g${S(2)}>${bx(300, 150, 180, 40, 'NEW: student passes', { c: 'amb', r: 4 })}${ar(296, 170, 186, 140, { c: 'a' })}${tx(390, 210, 'prioritised and inserted\ninto the stack', { c: 'xs hot' })}</g>
    <g${S(3)}>${tx(330, 290, 'dark mode removed: requirements can be re-prioritised or dropped at any time', { c: 'xs red' })}<line x1="24" y1="234" x2="176" y2="234" class="ln r"/></g>`),
    ['All requirements sit in one stack ordered by priority.', 'Each iteration implements the <b>highest-priority</b> requirements from the top.', 'Each new requirement is <b>prioritised and added</b> to the stack, not appended to a frozen SRS.', 'Requirements may be <b>re-prioritised</b> or <b>removed</b> at any time.']);
  H(L, `${VS([['Adoption detractors', '', ['Sketchy definitions → inconsistent, diverse interpretations', 'High-quality people skills required', 'Short iterations inhibit a long-term perspective', 'Feature creep: harder to manage customer expectations; cost, time and quality hard to quantify']], ['Shortcomings', '', ['Agility comes from <b>tacit knowledge</b> in the team, not formal documents: it can be misinterpreted', 'External review is difficult', 'When the project ends and the team disperses, <b>maintenance becomes difficult</b>']]])}`);

  H(L, `<h2>2 · Extreme Programming (XP)</h2>
    <p>Proposed by <b>Kent Beck in 1999</b>. The name comes from its idea: take the best practices to <b>extreme</b> levels. "If something is good, why not do it all the time?"</p>
    ${TBL(['If … is good', 'XP does it all the time'], [['code review', '<b>pair programming</b>: always review'], ['testing', '<b>test-driven development</b>: continually write and run tests'], ['incremental development', 'new increments every few days'], ['simplicity', 'the simplest design that supports only the currently required functionality'], ['design', 'everybody designs daily: <b>refactoring</b>'], ['architecture', 'everybody defines and refines the architecture: <b>metaphor</b>'], ['integration testing', 'build and integrate several times a day: <b>continuous integration</b>']])}
    ${COLS(`<h4>Four values</h4><ul><li><b>Communication</b> among the team and with customers</li><li><b>Simplicity</b>: build something simple that works today</li><li><b>Feedback</b>: a system kept away from users is trouble waiting to happen</li><li><b>Courage</b>: don't hesitate to discard code</li></ul>`, `<h4>Best practices</h4><ul><li><b>Coding</b>: utmost attention</li><li><b>Testing</b>: the primary means to a fault-free product</li><li><b>Listening</b> to customers</li><li><b>Designing</b>: or dependencies become too many to comprehend</li><li><b>Feedback</b>: to learn requirements</li></ul>`)}`);
  H(L, SVG(640, 200, `<circle cx="320" cy="100" r="70" class="bx s2"/>${tx(320, 100, 'every couple\nof days', { c: 'sm mu' })}
    ${bx(250, 6, 140, 36, 'write test cases\nfrom the user story', { c: 'red', lh: 12 })}${bx(420, 120, 160, 36, 'implement a quick\nand dirty feature', { c: 'grn', lh: 12 })}${bx(60, 120, 160, 36, 'customer feedback,\nalter, refactor', { c: 'blu', lh: 12 })}
    ${pth('M390 30 C 450 40, 490 70, 500 116', { c: 'flow' })}${pth('M420 150 C 330 190, 300 190, 222 150', { c: 'flow' })}${pth('M140 118 C 150 70, 190 40, 248 28', { c: 'flow' })}`));

  H(L, `<h2>3 · Scrum</h2>
    <p>Scrum is one of the agile processes: <b>self-organising teams</b>, the product progresses in a series of <b>month-long sprints</b>, and requirements are captured as items in a <b>product backlog</b>. The framework has three roles, four ceremonies and three artifacts:</p>
    ${TBL(['Roles', 'Ceremonies', 'Artifacts'], [['Product Owner', 'Sprint planning', 'Product backlog'], ['Scrum Master', 'Daily scrum', 'Sprint backlog'], ['Team', 'Sprint review · Sprint retrospective', 'Burndown chart']])}`);
  stepper(L, 'One Scrum sprint on the Saarthi app', SVG(660, 290, `
    ${bx(10, 110, 120, 70, 'Product\nbacklog', { c: 'blu', at: S(0) })}${ar(130, 145, 160, 145, { at: S(1) })}
    ${bx(162, 120, 110, 50, 'Sprint\nplanning', { c: 'pur', at: S(1) })}${ar(272, 145, 300, 145, { at: S(2) })}
    ${bx(302, 110, 110, 70, 'Sprint\nbacklog', { c: 'amb', at: S(2) })}
    <g${S(3)}><circle cx="500" cy="145" r="62" class="bx grn"/>${tx(500, 145, 'SPRINT\n~1 month', {})}</g>
    <g${S(4)}><circle cx="500" cy="62" r="24" class="bx red"/>${tx(500, 62, 'daily\nscrum', { c: 'xs', lh: 11 })}${pth('M476 72 C 440 100, 440 110, 448 116', { c: 'r flow', m: false })}${pth('M524 72 C 560 100, 560 110, 552 116', { c: 'r flow', m: false })}</g>
    ${ar(412, 145, 436, 145, { at: S(3) })}${ar(562, 145, 590, 145, { at: S(5) })}
    ${bx(592, 115, 62, 60, 'Product\nincre-\nment', { c: 'grn', at: S(5), lh: 13 })}
    ${bx(380, 236, 130, 40, 'Sprint review', { c: 'pur', at: S(6) })}${bx(530, 236, 120, 40, 'Retrospective', { c: 'pur', at: S(7) })}
    ${pth('M622 176 C 622 210, 520 216, 470 234', { at: S(6) })}${pth('M380 256 C 200 280, 80 240, 70 184', { c: 'dash', at: S(7) })}${tx(180, 268, 'next sprint', { c: 'xs mu', at: S(7) })}`, 'wide'),
    ['The <b>product backlog</b>: a prioritised list of all desired work, owned and prioritised by the <b>Product Owner</b> (Dev). Story-based items ("let a passenger buy a day pass") and task-based items ("improve exception handling"), typically kept in a spreadsheet.', '<b>Sprint planning</b>: the product owner and the team negotiate which backlog items the team will work on to meet the release goals; the Scrum master ensures the goals are realistic.', 'The result is the <b>sprint backlog</b>: a subset of product backlog items broken into tasks, <b>created by the team</b> and updated daily. Only the team may change it (add needed tasks, remove unnecessary ones); estimates are updated whenever there is new information.', 'The <b>sprint</b>: target duration one month. The increment is designed, coded and tested within it. <b>No changes are entertained during a sprint</b> and no outside influence may interfere with the team.', 'The <b>daily scrum</b>: every day, 15 minutes, standing up. Three questions: What did you do yesterday? What will you do today? What obstacles are in your way? It is <b>not</b> a problem-solving session and <b>not</b> a way to find out who is behind; members make commitments to each other, and the Scrum master tracks progress.', 'The sprint ends with a working <b>product increment</b>.', 'The <b>sprint review</b>: the team presents what it accomplished, typically a demo of new features. Informal (the "2-hour preparation rule"); participants: customers, management, product owner, team.', 'The <b>sprint retrospective</b> reflects on the process, and the next sprint starts from the re-prioritised backlog.'], { ms: 3800 });
  H(L, `${COLS(`<h4>Product Owner</h4><ul><li>Acts on behalf of the customers</li><li>Defines features; decides release date and content</li><li>Prioritises features by market value; adjusts every iteration</li><li>Accepts or rejects the work results</li></ul>`, `<h4>Scrum Master (≈ project manager)</h4><ul><li>Facilitates the process; <b>removes impediments</b></li><li>Keeps the team fully functional and productive</li><li>Enables cooperation across roles</li><li><b>Shields the team</b> from outside interference</li></ul>`, `<h4>Team</h4><ul><li>5-9 people (slides also say 5-10), <b>cross-functional</b>: programmers, testers, QA, UI designers</li><li><b>Self-organising</b></li><li>Membership changes only <b>between</b> sprints</li></ul>`)}
    <h3>Burndown charts</h3><p>Simple but effective information radiators. Three types: <b>sprint burndown</b> (total sprint-backlog hours remaining per day; ideally reaches zero at the end of the sprint; in reality not a straight line), <b>release burndown</b> (x-axis sprints, y-axis story points remaining: will the release be on time? how many more sprints?) and <b>product burndown</b> (the big picture across all releases).</p>`);
  stepper(L, 'Sprint burndown for Saarthi sprint 3', SVG(620, 250, `<line x1="60" y1="210" x2="600" y2="210" class="ln"/><line x1="60" y1="210" x2="60" y2="20" class="ln"/>${tx(330, 236, 'days of the sprint →', { c: 'xs mu' })}${tx(28, 110, 'hours\nleft', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    <line x1="60" y1="30" x2="590" y2="210" class="ln dash"/>${tx(450, 140, 'ideal', { c: 'xs mu' })}
    <polyline points="60,30 110,40 160,62 210,60 260,92" class="ln thick b"${S(1)}/><polyline points="260,92 310,118 360,112 410,140" class="ln thick b"${S(2)}/><polyline points="410,140 460,168 510,182 560,200 590,210" class="ln thick g"${S(3)}/>
    <g${S(2)}><circle cx="360" cy="112" r="6" class="fill-r"/>${tx(380, 96, 'new tasks found', { c: 'xs red' })}</g>`),
    ['Day 1: 600 task-hours in the sprint backlog. The dashed line is the ideal burn rate.', 'The team burns down unevenly; Riya can see at a glance whether they are above or below the ideal line.', 'Mid-sprint the team discovers extra tasks (the line goes up for a day). Only the team adds them; the sprint goal is unchanged.', 'The remaining work reaches zero at the end of the sprint: the increment is done. "In reality it is not a straight line."']);
  H(L, `<p><b>Scaling Scrum</b>: a typical team is 6-10 people, but Jeff Sutherland has run Scrum with over 800 people using a "<b>Scrum of Scrums</b>" (meta-Scrum); how often the teams meet depends on how tightly their work is coupled.</p>
    <h2>4 · Choosing the process</h2>
    <p>The right process depends on the characteristics of the <b>product</b>, the <b>development team</b> and the <b>customer</b>. Rules of thumb from the lecture: when uncertainty is high, favour an <b>evolutionary</b> approach; for well-understood applications, the <b>waterfall</b>; for novice team members, the <b>incremental</b> model. Saarthi uses four models at once:</p>
    ${TBL(['Saarthi part', 'Model', 'Why'], [['Depot portal', 'Waterfall', 'Stable, well-understood requirements; experienced team'], ['In-bus unit firmware', 'V-model', 'Embedded, high reliability, known requirements'], ['Passenger app, first months', 'Prototype → Scrum', 'Unclear requirements, then frequent change'], ['GPS in old-city lanes', 'Spiral', 'High technical risk to resolve first'], ['Depot reports', 'RAD', 'Non-critical, modular, tool support']])}`);
  choice(L, 'In Riya\'s first daily scrum, Arjun starts debugging a database deadlock with Pooja in front of everyone. As Scrum master, what should Riya do?', [
    ['Let them continue: solving problems is the point of the meeting.', 'The daily scrum is explicitly NOT a problem-solving session; it must stay at 15 minutes.', false],
    ['Note the obstacle, keep the scrum to the three questions, and help them solve it right after the meeting.', 'Right: the Scrum master removes impediments, but outside the 15-minute stand-up.', true],
    ['Use the meeting to find out who is behind schedule.', 'The daily scrum is not a way to collect information about who is behind.', false]]);
  quickcheck(L, [['Name the Scrum roles, ceremonies and artifacts.', 'Roles: product owner, Scrum master, team. Ceremonies: sprint planning, daily scrum, sprint review, sprint retrospective. Artifacts: product backlog, sprint backlog, burndown chart.'], ['Who prioritises the product backlog? Who updates the sprint backlog?', 'The product owner prioritises the product backlog; only the team updates the sprint backlog.'], ['What does XP turn code review and testing into?', 'Pair programming and test-driven development.']]);
  H(L, RECAP('Agile (mid-1990s): fit the process to the project, avoid waste, handle change; manifesto: individuals & interactions, working software, customer collaboration, responding to change.', 'One increment at a time, no long-term plans, release every iteration, face-to-face, small co-located teams (5-9), working software = progress; travel light on documents.', 'Weak points: tacit knowledge, hard external review, hard maintenance, feature creep, needs skilled people.', 'XP (Kent Beck, 1999): pair programming, TDD, continuous integration, refactoring, metaphor, simplest design; values communication, simplicity, feedback, courage.', 'Scrum: PO / Scrum master / team; sprint ≈ 1 month, no changes inside; daily scrum 15 min, 3 questions, not problem-solving; backlogs; burndown charts (sprint, release, product).', 'Process choice: high uncertainty → evolutionary; well understood → waterfall; novice team → incremental.'));
  H(L, NEXT('Before Sahyadri signs the contract, its finance head asks a blunt question: will Saarthi make money, for PCT and for us?', 't-bizcase', 'Chapter 3.1 · The business case'));
});
